{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"572a7bad4a2b391d1cbd2d6af35921046c4409c234ca4465fbb8a7398143bade","slug":"pages.m16-matched-pairs-6-of-8","location":"/pages/243-m16-matched-pairs-6-of-8.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"The Paired t-Test: Worked Examples","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"abbreviations":{"ANOVA":"Analysis of Variance","CI":"Confidence Interval","CLT":"Central Limit Theorem","EDA":"Exploratory Data Analysis","IQR":"Interquartile Range","SD":"Standard Deviation","SE":"Standard Error","SRS":"Simple Random Sample","INCREASE":null,"INCREASED":null,"DECREASES":null,"OBSERVED":null,"ROSE":null},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"243-m16-matched-pairs-6-of-8.md","url":"/textbook/build/243-m16-matched-pair-b87f20f1ed677ae8c7e9ba4d5c56e01b.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The “driving after having 2 beers” example is a case in which observations are paired by subject. In other words, both samples have the same subjects, so that each subject is measured twice. Typically, as in our example, one of the measurements occurs before a treatment/intervention (2 beers in our case), and the other measurement after the treatment/intervention. Our next example is another typical type of study where the matched pairs design is used—a study involving twins.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"G8HjuS2dZt"}],"key":"ObzSxeMNxs"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: IQ Scores of Twins Reared Apart","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"S46bgCaa5u"}],"key":"f77j1Yt1ya"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Researchers have long been interested in the extent to which intelligence, as measured by IQ score, is affected by “nurture” as opposed to “nature”: that is, are people’s IQ scores mainly a result of their upbringing and environment, or are they mainly an inherited trait? A study was designed to measure the effect of home environment on intelligence—more specifically, to address the question: “Are there significant differences in IQ scores between people who were raised by their birth parents and those who were raised by someone else?”","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"RyRntudMlg"}],"key":"YII98LjbNG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In order to answer this question, the researchers needed to get two groups of subjects (one from the population of people who were raised by their birth parents, and one from the population of people who were raised by someone else) who are as similar as possible in all other respects. In particular, since genetic differences may also affect intelligence, the researchers wanted to control for this confounding factor.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"JP6E66VNim"}],"key":"hdLvXjVMBk"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We know from our discussion on study design (in the Producing Data unit) that one way to (at least theoretically) control for all confounding factors is randomization—randomizing subjects to the different treatment groups. In this case, however, this is not possible. This is an observational study; you cannot randomize children to either be raised by their birth parents or to be raised by someone else. How else can we eliminate the genetics factor? We can conduct a “twin study.”","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"mqlVcIeTit"}],"key":"lGI5vFQOeO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because identical twins are genetically the same, a good design for obtaining information to answer this question would be to compare IQ scores for identical twins, one of whom was raised by birth parents and the other by someone else. Such a design (matched pairs) is an excellent way of making a comparison between individuals who differ only with respect to the explanatory variable of interest (upbringing) but are as alike as they can possibly be in all other important aspects (inborn intelligence). Identical twins reared apart were studied by Susan Farber, who published her studies in the book ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"jyYskRSVW4"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identical Twins Reared Apart","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"NoKKcfg3zy"}],"key":"dAdJeNBzgx"},{"type":"text","value":" (1981, Basic Books). In this problem, we use the IQ scores of 32 pairs of identical twins who were reared apart, as they appear in Farber’s book.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"OJ64h3WdOO"}],"key":"afkyuQiH4O"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here are the important things to note about the design:","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"nTqnUWZqEn"}],"key":"J7S02BYKDW"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We are essentially comparing the mean IQ scores in two populations that are defined by our (two-valued categorical) explanatory variable—upbringing (X), whose two values are: raised by birth parents, raised by someone else.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"uByOWSAFFT"}],"key":"RcesZv612F"}],"key":"IqPsf0ScVV"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is a matched pairs design (as opposed to a two independent samples design), since each observation in one sample is linked (matched) with an observation in the second sample. The observations are paired by twins.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"lSySOOkrZO"}],"key":"hWOujYthgj"}],"key":"NaRLcepGnf"}],"key":"aBTlYyCGWc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each of the 32 rows in the data set represents one pair of twins. Keeping the notation that we used above, twin 1 is the twin who was raised by his/her birth parents, and twin 2 is the twin who was raised by someone else. Let’s carry out the analysis.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"xgkQfE3AS4"}],"key":"RygYpVpIdS"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 1: Stating the hypotheses.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"yqyYJK7oUo"}],"key":"IdnCeOuhAF"},{"type":"text","value":" Recall that in matched pairs, we reduce the data from two samples to one sample of differences, and we state our hypotheses in terms of the mean of the differences, ","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"ewWIqQiRxV"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\mu_d","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_d</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3361em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">d</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"BkSIfONP6A"},{"type":"text","value":". Since we would like to test whether there are differences in IQ scores between people who were raised by their birth parents and those who weren’t, we are carrying out the two-sided test:","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"pqKexunOJA"}],"key":"Rn0cbMbXV1"},{"typ